// InfraOveragePolicy is the enforcement ladder applied to a plan's
// INFRASTRUCTURE dimensions (deployments, not daemons) once consumption
// crosses the included allowance.
//
// The ladder has four rungs, in order:
//
//	notify        at notify_threshold_percent of the allowance (default 80)
//	block_scale_up at 100% — the tenant keeps what is running, but may not
//	              add replicas, deployments or vClusters
//	throttle      after overage_grace_hours (default 72) of continuous
//	              overage — the workload is rate-limited, not stopped
//	suspend       ONLY when suspend_on_overage is explicitly true
//
// SUSPEND DEFAULTS TO FALSE, AND THAT INVERSION IS DELIBERATE. Daemon
// enforcement (internal/enforcement.Config.SuspendOnOverage) defaults to
// TRUE because a daemon is a session: suspending one costs its owner an
// interactive context they can rebuild. A deployment is a subscription —
// somebody's production service, possibly serving their own customers —
// and stopping it is an outage we chose to cause over a billing dispute.
// The default therefore flips: a customer must explicitly ask for a hard
// cap ("never let me be billed past X, even if that means downtime") for
// this to become true.
